# /etc/pam.d/sshd — sshd(8) SSH login.
#
# !!! TEMPORARY WIDE-OPEN DIAGNOSIS CONFIG — REVERT BEFORE REAL USE !!!
#
# Every facility is pam_permit.so, so sshd's keyboard-interactive auth
# succeeds with ZERO prompts: `ssh root@host` drops straight to a root
# shell with no password and no key. This is zero-credential root for
# anyone who can reach port 22 — acceptable ONLY on a trusted local test
# LAN, for diagnosing boot/network issues off the slow console.
#
# To restore security, revert this file to the Apple-source PAM stack
# (issue #97) and flip sshd_config back to a real policy:
#   auth     required  pam_unix.so   no_warn try_first_pass
#   account  required  pam_unix.so
#   session  required  pam_uwtmp.so
#   session  optional  pam_launchd.so   (keep it; see below)
#   password required  pam_unix.so   no_warn try_first_pass

auth		required	pam_permit.so
account		required	pam_permit.so
session		required	pam_permit.so
# pam_launchd moves the SSH session into the user's per-user launchd
# bootstrap (Background session), so processes started from the session
# look up Mach services the way they do on Darwin (nextbsd-userland#354).
# Darwin 10.9 lists it in sshd as `session required`; NextBSD uses
# `optional`. The module returns PAM_IGNORE whenever the per-user launchd
# is missing or fails its probes, and the session then stays on the root
# bootstrap. Darwin puts it ahead of pam_mount, which NextBSD does not ship;
# here it is the last session module.
# OpenPAM still rejects the whole sshd service if /usr/lib/pam_launchd.so.6
# cannot be loaded, so this line needs a userland that ships the module.
session		optional	pam_launchd.so
password	required	pam_permit.so
